-1
0
6
0
1
1
3
```

**Logic**

Two binary searches using the lower-bound form. The first occurrence is the lower bound of the target: the first index whose value is at least the target. The last occurrence is the lower bound of target + 1, minus one.

The key change from a plain binary search is that an equal comparison does not return; it keeps narrowing toward the side you want. Bolting a linear scan onto a plain search would degrade to O(n) on an array that is entirely the target, which is exactly the case the question is testing.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int[] SearchRange(int[] nums, int target)
    {
        int first = LowerBound(nums, target);

        // Not present at all: either past the end, or a different value sits there.
        if (first == nums.Length || nums[first] != target) return new[] { -1, -1 };

        int last = LowerBound(nums, target + 1) - 1;
        return new[] { first, last };
    }

    // First index whose value is >= target; nums.Length if there is none.
    private static int LowerBound(int[] nums, int target)
    {
        int low = 0, high = nums.Length;      // high is EXCLUSIVE here

        while (low < high)
        {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if (nums[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid;                   // equal keeps searching leftwards
        }

        return low;
    }

    private static string Show(int[] r) => $"[{r[0]},{r[1]}]";

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Show(SearchRange(new[] { 5, 7, 7, 8, 8, 10 }, 8)));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(SearchRange(new[] { 5, 7, 7, 8, 8, 10 }, 6)));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(SearchRange(new int[0], 0)));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(SearchRange(new[] { 1 }, 1)));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(SearchRange(new[] { 2, 2, 2, 2 }, 2)));   // entirely the target
        Console.WriteLine(Show(SearchRange(new[] { 1, 2, 3 }, 3)));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
[3,4]
[-1,-1]
[-1,-1]
[0,0]
[0,3]
[2,2]
```

**Logic**

The trap is doing it naively: zeroing a row immediately then reading those new zeros as if they were original, which cascades until the whole matrix is zero. So the positions must be recorded before any writing.

Using two sets of flags costs O(rows + cols) space. The O(1)-space version stores those flags in the matrix itself, using the first row and first column as the markers, with one extra variable for the first column because the cell at the origin serves both and would otherwise be ambiguous.

O(rows × cols) time.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static void SetZeroes(int[][] matrix)
    {
        if (matrix == null || matrix.Length == 0) return;

        int rows = matrix.Length, cols = matrix[0].Length;

        // matrix[0][0] would mean both "row 0 has a zero" and "col 0 has a zero",
        // so the first column gets its own flag.
        bool firstColumnHasZero = false;

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
        {
            if (matrix[r][0] == 0) firstColumnHasZero = true;

            for (int c = 1; c < cols; c++)
                if (matrix[r][c] == 0)
                {
                    matrix[r][0] = 0;    // mark the row
                    matrix[0][c] = 0;    // mark the column
                }
        }

        // Apply, working backwards so the markers are read before being overwritten.
        for (int r = rows - 1; r >= 0; r--)
        {
            for (int c = cols - 1; c >= 1; c--)
                if (matrix[r][0] == 0 || matrix[0][c] == 0) matrix[r][c] = 0;

            if (firstColumnHasZero) matrix[r][0] = 0;
        }
    }

    private static string Render(int[][] m) =>
        string.Join(" / ", Array.ConvertAll(m, r => string.Join(",", r)));

    private static string Zeroed(params int[][] m)
    {
        SetZeroes(m);
        return Render(m);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Zeroed(
            new[] { 1, 1, 1 }, new[] { 1, 0, 1 }, new[] { 1, 1, 1 }));

        Console.WriteLine(Zeroed(
            new[] { 0, 1, 2, 0 }, new[] { 3, 4, 5, 2 }, new[] { 1, 3, 1, 5 }));

        Console.WriteLine(Zeroed(new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 3, 4 }));   // no zeros
        Console.WriteLine(Zeroed(new[] { 0 }));
        Console.WriteLine(Zeroed(new[] { 1, 0 }, new[] { 1, 1 }));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
1,0,1 / 0,0,0 / 1,0,1
0,0,0,0 / 0,4,5,0 / 0,3,1,0
1,2 / 3,4
0
0,0 / 1,0
```

**Logic**

Maintain four boundaries: top, bottom, left and right. Walk the top row left to right and lower the top boundary; walk the right column downward and pull the right boundary in; then the bottom row right to left, and the left column upward, adjusting each boundary as it is consumed.

The subtlety is the two return legs. After the first two walks the layer may be exhausted, so the bottom and left walks must re-check that the boundaries have not crossed, otherwise a single row or column is visited twice.

O(rows × cols) time, O(1) extra space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static IList<int> SpiralOrder(int[][] matrix)
    {
        var result = new List<int>();
        if (matrix == null || matrix.Length == 0) return result;

        int top = 0, bottom = matrix.Length - 1;
        int left = 0, right = matrix[0].Length - 1;

        while (top <= bottom && left <= right)
        {
            for (int c = left; c <= right; c++) result.Add(matrix[top][c]);
            top++;

            for (int r = top; r <= bottom; r++) result.Add(matrix[r][right]);
            right--;

            // Re-check: the layer may already be finished after the two walks above.
            if (top <= bottom)
            {
                for (int c = right; c >= left; c--) result.Add(matrix[bottom][c]);
                bottom--;
            }

            if (left <= right)
            {
                for (int r = bottom; r >= top; r--) result.Add(matrix[r][left]);
                left++;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SpiralOrder(new[] {
            new[] { 1, 2, 3 }, new[] { 4, 5, 6 }, new[] { 7, 8, 9 } })));

        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SpiralOrder(new[] {
            new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4 }, new[] { 5, 6, 7, 8 }, new[] { 9, 10, 11, 12 } })));

        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SpiralOrder(new[] { new[] { 1, 2, 3 } })));  // one row
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SpiralOrder(new[] {
            new[] { 1 }, new[] { 2 }, new[] { 3 } })));                                  // one column
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SpiralOrder(new[] { new[] { 7 } })));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
1,2,3,6,9,8,7,4,5
1,2,3,4,8,12,11,10,9,5,6,7
1,2,3
1,2,3
7
```

**Logic**

Transpose, then reverse each row. Transposing reflects across the main diagonal, and reversing each row reflects horizontally; the two reflections compose into a 90-degree clockwise rotation.

Transpose only the upper triangle, swapping `[r][c]` with `[c][r]` for `c > r`. Covering the whole matrix would swap every pair twice and leave it unchanged, which is the classic bug here.

For anticlockwise, reverse each column instead, or equivalently reverse the row order before transposing.

O(n²) time, O(1) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static void Rotate(int[][] matrix)
    {
        int n = matrix.Length;

        // 1. Transpose: only the upper triangle, or every swap happens twice.
        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++)
            for (int c = r + 1; c < n; c++)
                (matrix[r][c], matrix[c][r]) = (matrix[c][r], matrix[r][c]);

        // 2. Reverse each row.
        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++)
            Array.Reverse(matrix[r]);
    }

    // Anticlockwise: transpose, then reverse the row ORDER instead.
    public static void RotateCounterClockwise(int[][] matrix)
    {
        int n = matrix.Length;

        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++)
            for (int c = r + 1; c < n; c++)
                (matrix[r][c], matrix[c][r]) = (matrix[c][r], matrix[r][c]);

        Array.Reverse(matrix);
    }

    private static string Render(int[][] m) =>
        string.Join(" / ", Array.ConvertAll(m, r => string.Join(",", r)));

    public static void Main()
    {
        var a = new[] { new[] { 1, 2, 3 }, new[] { 4, 5, 6 }, new[] { 7, 8, 9 } };
        Rotate(a);
        Console.WriteLine(Render(a));

        var b = new[] { new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 3, 4 } };
        Rotate(b);
        Console.WriteLine(Render(b));

        var c = new[] { new[] { 5 } };
        Rotate(c);
        Console.WriteLine(Render(c));

        var d = new[] { new[] { 1, 2, 3 }, new[] { 4, 5, 6 }, new[] { 7, 8, 9 } };
        RotateCounterClockwise(d);
        Console.WriteLine(Render(d));

        // Four rotations return to the original.
        var e = new[] { new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 3, 4 } };
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) Rotate(e);
        Console.WriteLine(Render(e));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
7,4,1 / 8,5,2 / 9,6,3
3,1 / 4,2
5
3,6,9 / 2,5,8 / 1,4,7
1,2 / 3,4
```

**Logic**

Unlike the maximum sum, a product can flip sign, so the smallest value so far matters: multiplying a large negative by another negative produces a large positive. Track both the running maximum and the running minimum ending at each position.

When the current element is negative, the roles swap, so compute the candidates from both before assigning. Failing to do that, and using the already-updated maximum when computing the minimum, is the standard bug.

A zero resets both, since any product through it is zero. O(n) time, O(1) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int MaxProduct(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length == 0) return 0;

        int best = nums[0];
        int currentMax = nums[0], currentMin = nums[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            int n = nums[i];

            // Compute both candidates BEFORE assigning, or currentMin uses a
            // currentMax that has already been overwritten.
            int candidateMax = Math.Max(n, Math.Max(currentMax * n, currentMin * n));
            int candidateMin = Math.Min(n, Math.Min(currentMax * n, currentMin * n));

            currentMax = candidateMax;
            currentMin = candidateMin;

            best = Math.Max(best, currentMax);
        }

        return best;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProduct(new[] { 2, 3, -2, 4 }));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProduct(new[] { -2, 0, -1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProduct(new[] { -2, 3, -4 }));    // two negatives combine
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProduct(new[] { -3 }));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProduct(new[] { 0, 2 }));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProduct(new[] { -1, -2, -9, -6 }));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
6
0
24
-3
2
108
```

The `-2, 3, -4` case is the point: the answer uses the whole array, because two negatives multiply to a positive, which no maximum-only algorithm would find.

**Logic**

Two pointers from both ends moving inward. Skip anything that is not a letter or digit, compare the rest case-insensitively, and stop when the pointers meet. O(n) time and O(1) extra space.

The tempting alternative, filtering into a new string and comparing it to its reverse, is simple to write but allocates two extra strings. The two-pointer version allocates nothing, which is the reason it is the expected answer.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool IsPalindrome(string s)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return true;

        int left = 0, right = s.Length - 1;

        while (left < right)
        {
            // Skip the characters the problem says do not count.
            while (left < right && !char.IsLetterOrDigit(s[left])) left++;
            while (left < right && !char.IsLetterOrDigit(s[right])) right--;

            if (char.ToLowerInvariant(s[left]) != char.ToLowerInvariant(s[right]))
                return false;

            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return true;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(IsPalindrome("A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsPalindrome("race a car"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsPalindrome(""));
        Console.WriteLine(IsPalindrome(".,"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsPalindrome("0P"));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
True
False
True
True
False
```

The last case is the one that catches naive solutions: `'0'` and `'P'` differ by 32 in ASCII, so code that lowercases by flipping a bit or by adding 32 wrongly reports them equal.

**Logic**

Two strings are anagrams when they contain the same characters with the same counts. Different lengths mean an immediate no. Otherwise count each character of the first string, then decrement for each character of the second; if any count goes negative, or the strings differ in length, they are not anagrams. One pass over each string, O(n) time.

Sorting both and comparing also works and is a fine answer to give, but it is O(n log n) and allocates. Counting is O(n), and over a fixed alphabet the counter is a small fixed array rather than a dictionary.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool IsAnagram(string s, string t)
    {
        if (s == null || t == null) return s == t;
        if (s.Length != t.Length) return false;   // cheapest possible rejection

        var counts = new Dictionary<char, int>();

        foreach (char c in s)
            counts[c] = counts.TryGetValue(c, out int n) ? n + 1 : 1;

        foreach (char c in t)
        {
            if (!counts.TryGetValue(c, out int n) || n == 0) return false;
            counts[c] = n - 1;
        }

        // Lengths are equal and nothing went negative, so every count is back to zero.
        return true;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(IsAnagram("anagram", "nagaram"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsAnagram("rat", "car"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsAnagram("a", "ab"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsAnagram("", ""));
        Console.WriteLine(IsAnagram("aacc", "ccac"));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
True
False
False
True
False
```

The final case is the one that breaks solutions which only check that both strings use the same set of characters without comparing counts.

**Logic**

A sliding window with a map from character to its last index. Extend the window to the right one character at a time; when the incoming character was seen inside the current window, jump the left edge to just past that previous occurrence. Record the best width as you go. Each character is visited once, so O(n) time and O(k) space where k is the alphabet size.

The important detail is the phrase *inside the current window*. A previous index that lies before the left edge is stale and must be ignored, otherwise the window moves backwards and the answer is too large.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int LengthOfLongestSubstring(string s)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return 0;

        var lastSeen = new Dictionary<char, int>();
        int best = 0, left = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < s.Length; right++)
        {
            char c = s[right];

            // Only move left forward, never backward: an older index outside the
            // window has already been excluded and must not pull it back.
            if (lastSeen.TryGetValue(c, out int prev) && prev >= left)
                left = prev + 1;

            lastSeen[c] = right;
            best = Math.Max(best, right - left + 1);
        }

        return best;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(LengthOfLongestSubstring("abcabcbb"));  // "abc"
        Console.WriteLine(LengthOfLongestSubstring("bbbbb"));      // "b"
        Console.WriteLine(LengthOfLongestSubstring("pwwkew"));     // "wke"
        Console.WriteLine(LengthOfLongestSubstring(""));
        Console.WriteLine(LengthOfLongestSubstring("abba"));       // the stale-index trap
        Console.WriteLine(LengthOfLongestSubstring("dvdf"));       // "vdf"
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
3
1
3
0
2
3
```

`"abba"` is the case that exposes the bug: without the `prev >= left` guard the left edge jumps back to index 1 when the final `a` arrives, and the answer comes out as 3 instead of 2.

**Logic**

Give every string a canonical key that is identical for anagrams and different otherwise, then group on it in one pass with a dictionary. Sorting the characters is the simplest key, at O(k log k) per string. A 26-slot character count rendered to a string is an O(k) key and avoids the sort.

Total cost is O(n·k log k) with the sorted key, versus O(n²·k) for comparing every pair, which is the point of the question.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;

public class Solution
{
    public static IList<IList<string>> GroupAnagrams(string[] strs)
    {
        var groups = new Dictionary<string, IList<string>>();

        foreach (string s in strs)
        {
            string key = CountKey(s);
            if (!groups.TryGetValue(key, out var bucket))
                groups[key] = bucket = new List<string>();
            bucket.Add(s);
        }

        return groups.Values.ToList();
    }

    // O(k) key: counts of each letter, not a sort. "#" separates the counts so
    // that 1,11 and 11,1 cannot collide into the same string.
    private static string CountKey(string s)
    {
        var counts = new int[26];
        foreach (char c in s) counts[c - 'a']++;

        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) sb.Append(counts[i]).Append('#');
        return sb.ToString();
    }

    // The simpler key, for comparison: O(k log k) but three lines.
    private static string SortKey(string s)
    {
        var chars = s.ToCharArray();
        Array.Sort(chars);
        return new string(chars);
    }

    private static string Render(IList<IList<string>> groups) =>
        string.Join(" | ", groups.Select(g => "[" + string.Join(",", g) + "]"));

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Render(GroupAnagrams(new[] { "eat", "tea", "tan", "ate", "nat", "bat" })));
        Console.WriteLine(Render(GroupAnagrams(new[] { "" })));
        Console.WriteLine(Render(GroupAnagrams(new[] { "a" })));
        Console.WriteLine(Render(GroupAnagrams(new[] { "abc", "cba", "bac", "xyz" })));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
[eat,tea,ate] | [tan,nat] | [bat]
[]
[a]
[abc,cba,bac] | [xyz]
```

**Logic**

Expand around every possible centre. A palindrome is defined by its centre, and there are 2n-1 of them: n single characters for odd-length palindromes, and n-1 gaps between characters for even-length ones. From each centre, expand outward while the characters match and record the longest span seen. O(n²) time, O(1) space.

The common mistake is only checking odd centres, which misses `"abba"` entirely. The DP table is also O(n²) but uses O(n²) space, so expansion is strictly better.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static string LongestPalindrome(string s)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return "";

        int bestStart = 0, bestLength = 1;

        for (int centre = 0; centre < s.Length; centre++)
        {
            Expand(s, centre, centre, ref bestStart, ref bestLength);       // odd length
            Expand(s, centre, centre + 1, ref bestStart, ref bestLength);   // even length
        }

        return s.Substring(bestStart, bestLength);
    }

    private static void Expand(string s, int left, int right, ref int bestStart, ref int bestLength)
    {
        while (left >= 0 && right < s.Length && s[left] == s[right])
        {
            left--;
            right++;
        }

        // The loop exits one step past the match, so the span is (left+1 .. right-1).
        int length = right - left - 1;
        if (length > bestLength)
        {
            bestLength = length;
            bestStart = left + 1;
        }
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(LongestPalindrome("babad"));   // "bab" or "aba"
        Console.WriteLine(LongestPalindrome("cbbd"));    // even-length centre
        Console.WriteLine(LongestPalindrome("a"));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestPalindrome(""));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestPalindrome("ac"));      // no palindrome longer than 1
        Console.WriteLine(LongestPalindrome("aaaa"));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestPalindrome("forgeeksskeegfor"));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
bab
bb
a

a
aaaa
geeksskeeg
```

**Logic**

A stack, because the closing bracket must match the most recently opened one, which is last-in-first-out by definition. Push every opening bracket; on a closing bracket, the stack must be non-empty and its top must be the matching opener, so pop and compare. At the end the stack must be empty, otherwise something was never closed. O(n) time, O(n) space in the worst case of all openers.

The two failure modes to handle explicitly are a closing bracket arriving when the stack is empty, and leftovers at the end. Solutions that only count brackets pass `([)]`, which is why counting is not enough.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool IsValid(string s)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return true;

        var pairs = new Dictionary<char, char> { [')'] = '(', [']'] = '[', ['}'] = '{' };
        var stack = new Stack<char>();

        foreach (char c in s)
        {
            if (pairs.ContainsValue(c))
            {
                stack.Push(c);
            }
            else if (pairs.TryGetValue(c, out char opener))
            {
                // A closer with nothing open, or the wrong thing open, is invalid.
                if (stack.Count == 0 || stack.Pop() != opener) return false;
            }
        }

        return stack.Count == 0;   // anything still open was never closed
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(IsValid("()"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsValid("()[]{}"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsValid("(]"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsValid("([)]"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsValid("{[]}"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsValid("("));
        Console.WriteLine(IsValid(")"));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
True
True
False
False
True
False
False
```

**Logic**

The algorithm is easy; the edge cases are the question. In order: skip leading whitespace, read an optional single sign, consume digits until a non-digit, and clamp to int.MinValue or int.MaxValue on overflow rather than wrapping. Anything unparseable yields 0.

Overflow is the part that separates answers. Detect it *before* multiplying, by checking whether the accumulated value already exceeds `int.MaxValue / 10`, or accumulate in a long and clamp. Multiplying first and checking afterwards is already too late, because the overflow has happened.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int MyAtoi(string s)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return 0;

        int i = 0;
        while (i < s.Length && s[i] == ' ') i++;          // 1. leading whitespace only

        if (i == s.Length) return 0;

        int sign = 1;                                     // 2. one optional sign
        if (s[i] == '+' || s[i] == '-')
        {
            sign = s[i] == '-' ? -1 : 1;
            i++;
        }

        long value = 0;                                   // 3. digits, clamping as we go
        while (i < s.Length && char.IsAsciiDigit(s[i]))
        {
            value = value * 10 + (s[i] - '0');
            i++;

            // Clamp early: a long cannot absorb an arbitrarily long digit run either.
            if (sign == 1 && value > int.MaxValue) return int.MaxValue;
            if (sign == -1 && -value < int.MinValue) return int.MinValue;
        }

        return (int)(sign * value);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(MyAtoi("42"));
        Console.WriteLine(MyAtoi("   -42"));
        Console.WriteLine(MyAtoi("4193 with words"));      // stops at the space
        Console.WriteLine(MyAtoi("words and 987"));        // never starts
        Console.WriteLine(MyAtoi("-91283472332"));         // clamps to int.MinValue
        Console.WriteLine(MyAtoi("91283472332"));          // clamps to int.MaxValue
        Console.WriteLine(MyAtoi("+-12"));                 // two signs: invalid
        Console.WriteLine(MyAtoi("00000123"));             // leading zeros
        Console.WriteLine(MyAtoi(""));
        Console.WriteLine(MyAtoi("3.14"));                 // stops at the dot
    }
}
