**Logic**

Prefix sums with a hash map. If the running sum up to index j is `S`, then a subarray ending at j sums to k exactly when some earlier prefix equalled `S - k`. So keep a map from prefix sum to how many times it has occurred, and at each step add the count of `S - k`.

Seed the map with `{0: 1}` to represent the empty prefix, which is what lets a subarray starting at index 0 be counted. O(n) time, O(n) space.

The sliding window does not work here, because negative numbers mean the running sum is not monotonic and shrinking the window can increase the sum.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int SubarraySum(int[] nums, int k)
    {
        // {0:1} is the empty prefix: it makes a subarray starting at index 0 countable.
        var seen = new Dictionary<long, int> { [0] = 1 };
        long sum = 0;
        int count = 0;

        foreach (int n in nums)
        {
            sum += n;

            // Any earlier prefix equal to sum-k closes a subarray summing to k here.
            if (seen.TryGetValue(sum - k, out int times)) count += times;

            seen[sum] = seen.TryGetValue(sum, out int c) ? c + 1 : 1;
        }

        return count;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { 1, 1, 1 }, 2));
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { 1, 2, 3 }, 3));         // [1,2] and [3]
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { 1 }, 0));
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { -1, -1, 1 }, 0));       // negatives
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { 3, 4, 7, 2, -3, 1, 4, 2 }, 7));
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { 0, 0, 0 }, 0));         // every subarray counts
    }
}
```

