2
0
1
4
6
```

**Logic**

Put everything in a hash set, then for each value check whether it is the *start* of a run by asking if `value - 1` is absent. Only from a genuine start do you walk upward counting. That test is what keeps it linear: without it, a long run would be walked from every one of its members, making it quadratic.

Each value is visited at most twice overall, once in the outer loop and once during the single walk of its run, so O(n) time and O(n) space. Sorting would be O(n log n), which the problem is asking you to beat.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int LongestConsecutive(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length == 0) return 0;

        var set = new HashSet<int>(nums);   // also removes duplicates
        int best = 0;

        foreach (int n in set)
        {
            // Only start counting from the beginning of a run.
            if (set.Contains(n - 1)) continue;

            int current = n, length = 1;
            while (set.Contains(current + 1)) { current++; length++; }

            if (length > best) best = length;
        }

        return best;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new[] { 100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2 }));   // 1,2,3,4
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new[] { 0, 3, 7, 2, 5, 8, 4, 6, 0, 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new int[0]));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new[] { 5 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new[] { 1, 1, 1 }));      // duplicates collapse
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new[] { -3, -2, -1, 0 })); // negatives
    }
}
```

