7
```

**Logic**

At each position you may take one digit, if it is not zero, or two digits, if they form a number between 10 and 26. So `ways[i] = ways[i-1] (if valid single) + ways[i-2] (if valid pair)`, which is Fibonacci with validity conditions.

Zero is the whole difficulty. A zero cannot stand alone, so it must pair with the digit before it, and that pair must be 10 or 20; anything else makes the string undecodable and the count zero.

Two rolling variables give O(n) time and O(1) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int NumDecodings(string s)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s) || s[0] == '0') return 0;

        int twoBack = 1;   // the empty prefix: one way to decode nothing
        int oneBack = 1;   // the first character, already known not to be '0'

        for (int i = 1; i < s.Length; i++)
        {
            int current = 0;

            // Single digit: valid unless it is zero.
            if (s[i] != '0') current += oneBack;

            // Pair with the previous digit: valid only in 10..26.
            int pair = (s[i - 1] - '0') * 10 + (s[i] - '0');
            if (pair >= 10 && pair <= 26) current += twoBack;

            if (current == 0) return 0;   // this position cannot be decoded at all

            twoBack = oneBack;
            oneBack = current;
        }

        return oneBack;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(NumDecodings("12"));      // AB or L
        Console.WriteLine(NumDecodings("226"));     // BZ, VF, BBF
        Console.WriteLine(NumDecodings("06"));      // leading zero
        Console.WriteLine(NumDecodings("10"));      // only J
        Console.WriteLine(NumDecodings("100"));     // the second zero has no partner
        Console.WriteLine(NumDecodings("27"));      // 27 > 26, so only B G
        Console.WriteLine(NumDecodings("11106"));
    }
}
```

