**Logic**

Walk from the least significant digit. A digit below 9 can simply be incremented and the work is done. A 9 becomes 0 and the carry continues left.

If the loop finishes with a carry still outstanding, every digit was 9, so the result is one digit longer: a leading 1 followed by zeros. Allocating a new array of length n+1 gives zeros for free, so only the first element needs setting.

O(n) worst case, but O(1) whenever the last digit is not 9, which is nine times out of ten on random input.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int[] PlusOne(int[] digits)
    {
        if (digits == null || digits.Length == 0) return new[] { 1 };

        for (int i = digits.Length - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            if (digits[i] < 9)
            {
                digits[i]++;
                return digits;      // no carry: done immediately
            }

            digits[i] = 0;          // 9 becomes 0 and the carry moves left
        }

        // Every digit was 9: the answer is one digit longer, e.g. 999 -> 1000.
        var result = new int[digits.Length + 1];
        result[0] = 1;              // the rest default to 0
        return result;
    }

    private static string Inc(params int[] d) => string.Join("", PlusOne(d));

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Inc(1, 2, 3));
        Console.WriteLine(Inc(4, 3, 2, 1));
        Console.WriteLine(Inc(9));            // grows
        Console.WriteLine(Inc(9, 9, 9));      // grows
        Console.WriteLine(Inc(1, 9, 9));      // carry stops partway
        Console.WriteLine(Inc(0));
    }
}
