True
False
True
False
True
```

`catsandog` is the case that defeats greedy matching: taking "cats" then "and" leaves "og", and only backtracking to "cat" then "sand" reaches "dog" — which also fails, hence False.

**Logic**

A peak is any element greater than both neighbours, with the array treated as bounded by negative infinity at both ends. Binary search works even though the array is unsorted: compare the midpoint with its right neighbour, and move toward whichever side is ascending, because an ascending direction must eventually reach a peak before running off the end.

That argument is the whole answer. It guarantees a peak exists in the chosen half, so the search never discards all peaks. O(log n) time, O(1) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int FindPeakElement(int[] nums)
    {
        int low = 0, high = nums.Length - 1;

        while (low < high)
        {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            // Ascending to the right: a peak must lie that way.
            if (nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1]) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid;    // descending (or equal): mid could be the peak
        }

        return low;             // low == high, and that position is a peak
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        int[] a = { 1, 2, 3, 1 };
        Console.WriteLine(FindPeakElement(a));             // index 2

        int[] b = { 1, 2, 1, 3, 5, 6, 4 };
        int peak = FindPeakElement(b);
        // Several peaks exist; assert the answer really is one rather than a fixed index.
        bool valid = (peak == 0 || b[peak] > b[peak - 1])
                  && (peak == b.Length - 1 || b[peak] > b[peak + 1]);
        Console.WriteLine(valid);

        Console.WriteLine(FindPeakElement(new[] { 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(FindPeakElement(new[] { 2, 1 }));       // descending: index 0
        Console.WriteLine(FindPeakElement(new[] { 1, 2 }));       // ascending: index 1
        Console.WriteLine(FindPeakElement(new[] { 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 }));
    }
}
