**Logic**

Sorted input makes the hash map unnecessary. Two pointers start at both ends: if the sum is too large the right pointer moves left, reducing it; if too small the left pointer moves right, increasing it. The sorted order guarantees each move is in the right direction, and the pointers never need to back up.

O(n) time and O(1) space, against O(n) space for the hash-map version. Note this problem conventionally returns 1-based indices, which is a detail worth confirming rather than assuming.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    // Returns 1-based indices, as this problem conventionally specifies.
    public static int[] TwoSum(int[] numbers, int target)
    {
        if (numbers == null || numbers.Length < 2) return Array.Empty<int>();

        int left = 0, right = numbers.Length - 1;

        while (left < right)
        {
            int sum = numbers[left] + numbers[right];

            if (sum == target) return new[] { left + 1, right + 1 };

            if (sum < target) left++;    // need a larger sum
            else right--;                // need a smaller one
        }

        return Array.Empty<int>();       // no pair found
    }

    private static string Show(int[] r) => r.Length == 0 ? "(none)" : string.Join(",", r);

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Show(TwoSum(new[] { 2, 7, 11, 15 }, 9)));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(TwoSum(new[] { 2, 3, 4 }, 6)));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(TwoSum(new[] { -1, 0 }, -1)));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(TwoSum(new[] { 1, 2, 3 }, 100)));    // no pair
        Console.WriteLine(Show(TwoSum(new[] { 1, 1 }, 2)));         // duplicates
        Console.WriteLine(Show(TwoSum(new[] { 5 }, 5)));            // too short
    }
}
```

