10
0
3
3
3
```

**Logic**

Two passes. Count every character, then scan the string again in order and return the index of the first character whose count is 1. The second pass must walk the original string rather than the counts, because a dictionary has no meaningful order. O(n) time, O(k) space for the alphabet.

Doing it in one pass is not possible in general: you cannot know a character is unique until the whole string has been read.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int FirstUniqChar(string s)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return -1;

        var counts = new Dictionary<char, int>();
        foreach (char c in s)
            counts[c] = counts.TryGetValue(c, out int n) ? n + 1 : 1;

        // Second pass over the STRING, not the dictionary: order matters.
        for (int i = 0; i < s.Length; i++)
            if (counts[s[i]] == 1) return i;

        return -1;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(FirstUniqChar("leetcode"));
        Console.WriteLine(FirstUniqChar("loveleetcode"));
        Console.WriteLine(FirstUniqChar("aabb"));        // none unique
        Console.WriteLine(FirstUniqChar("z"));
        Console.WriteLine(FirstUniqChar(""));
        Console.WriteLine(FirstUniqChar("aadadaad"));
    }
}
```

