(empty)
[1]
[1] | [2] | [3]
```

**Logic**

The BST property is global, not local: every node in the left subtree must be less than the node, not merely its immediate child. So checking `left.Val < node.Val < right.Val` at each node is wrong, and it is the classic failure of this question.

Carry a valid range down instead. The root may be anything; moving left tightens the upper bound to the current value, moving right tightens the lower bound. A node outside its inherited range fails. O(n) time, O(h) stack space.

The alternative is an in-order traversal, which must produce strictly increasing values.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class TreeNode
{
    public int Val;
    public TreeNode Left, Right;
    public TreeNode(int val = 0, TreeNode left = null, TreeNode right = null)
    { Val = val; Left = left; Right = right; }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static bool IsValidBst(TreeNode root) => Validate(root, null, null);

    // long? bounds rather than int: a node holding int.MinValue is legal data and
    // must not be confused with "no bound set".
    private static bool Validate(TreeNode node, long? min, long? max)
    {
        if (node == null) return true;

        if (min.HasValue && node.Val <= min.Value) return false;
        if (max.HasValue && node.Val >= max.Value) return false;

        return Validate(node.Left, min, node.Val)     // left: tighten the upper bound
            && Validate(node.Right, node.Val, max);   // right: tighten the lower bound
    }

    // In-order must be strictly increasing. Iterative, so no stack-overflow risk.
    public static bool IsValidBstInOrder(TreeNode root)
    {
        var stack = new Stack<TreeNode>();
        long? previous = null;
        var current = root;

        while (current != null || stack.Count > 0)
        {
            while (current != null) { stack.Push(current); current = current.Left; }
            current = stack.Pop();

            if (previous.HasValue && current.Val <= previous.Value) return false;
            previous = current.Val;

            current = current.Right;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        var valid = new TreeNode(2, new TreeNode(1), new TreeNode(3));
        Console.WriteLine(IsValidBst(valid));

        //   5
        //  / \
        // 1   4      <- 4 is less than 5 but sits in the right subtree
        //    / \
        //   3   6
        var invalid = new TreeNode(5,
            new TreeNode(1),
            new TreeNode(4, new TreeNode(3), new TreeNode(6)));
        Console.WriteLine(IsValidBst(invalid));

        //   10
        //  /
        // 5
        //   \
        //    12   <- locally fine, globally invalid: 12 > 10 in the left subtree
        var sneaky = new TreeNode(10, new TreeNode(5, null, new TreeNode(12)), null);
        Console.WriteLine(IsValidBst(sneaky));
        Console.WriteLine(IsValidBstInOrder(sneaky));

        Console.WriteLine(IsValidBst(null));
        Console.WriteLine(IsValidBst(new TreeNode(int.MinValue)));   // the sentinel trap
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
True
False
False
False
True
True
```

**Logic**

The BST ordering does the work. Starting at the root: if both targets are smaller than the current node, the answer must be in the left subtree; if both are larger, it is in the right. The moment they diverge, or one equals the current node, that node is the lowest common ancestor, because it is the first point where their paths split.

No recursion or extra storage is needed, just a walk down one path: O(h) time, O(1) space. On a balanced tree that is O(log n).

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class TreeNode
{
    public int Val;
    public TreeNode Left, Right;
    public TreeNode(int val = 0, TreeNode left = null, TreeNode right = null)
    { Val = val; Left = left; Right = right; }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static TreeNode LowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, int p, int q)
    {
        var node = root;

        while (node != null)
        {
            if (p < node.Val && q < node.Val)        // both on the left
                node = node.Left;
            else if (p > node.Val && q > node.Val)   // both on the right
                node = node.Right;
            else
                return node;                         // paths split here, or one IS here
        }

        return null;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        //       6
        //     /   \
        //    2     8
        //   / \   / \
        //  0   4 7   9
        //     / \
        //    3   5
        var root = new TreeNode(6,
            new TreeNode(2, new TreeNode(0),
                new TreeNode(4, new TreeNode(3), new TreeNode(5))),
            new TreeNode(8, new TreeNode(7), new TreeNode(9)));

        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 2, 8).Val);   // splits at the root
        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 2, 4).Val);   // an ancestor IS a target
        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 3, 5).Val);
        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 7, 9).Val);
        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 0, 5).Val);
        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 6, 6).Val);   // same node twice
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
6
2
4
8
2
6
```

**Logic**

Without an ordering you cannot pick a direction, so search both subtrees and let the results tell you where the targets are. At each node: if the node itself is one of the targets, return it. Otherwise recurse both ways. If both sides return something, the targets lie on opposite sides and this node is the lowest common ancestor. If only one side returns, pass that result up.

One post-order pass, O(n) time, O(h) stack space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class TreeNode
{
    public int Val;
    public TreeNode Left, Right;
    public TreeNode(int val = 0, TreeNode left = null, TreeNode right = null)
    { Val = val; Left = left; Right = right; }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static TreeNode LowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, int p, int q)
    {
        if (root == null) return null;

        // A target found here: report it upward. A node counts as its own ancestor.
        if (root.Val == p || root.Val == q) return root;

        var left = LowestCommonAncestor(root.Left, p, q);
        var right = LowestCommonAncestor(root.Right, p, q);

        if (left != null && right != null) return root;   // they split here

        return left ?? right;                             // both on one side, or neither
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        //        3
        //      /   \
        //     5     1
        //    / \   / \
        //   6   2 0   8
        //      / \
        //     7   4
        var root = new TreeNode(3,
            new TreeNode(5, new TreeNode(6),
                new TreeNode(2, new TreeNode(7), new TreeNode(4))),
            new TreeNode(1, new TreeNode(0), new TreeNode(8)));

        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 5, 1).Val);
        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 5, 4).Val);   // ancestor is a target
        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 7, 4).Val);
        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 6, 8).Val);
        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 7, 8).Val);
        Console.WriteLine(LowestCommonAncestor(root, 6, 2).Val);
    }
}
```

