3
1,3
7
(empty)
4
```

**Logic**

Scan the grid. Every time an unvisited land cell is found, it must belong to a new island, so increment the count and flood the whole connected region so it is never counted again. The flood can be DFS or BFS; both visit each cell once, giving O(rows × cols) time.

Sinking visited land by overwriting it with water avoids a separate visited array. That mutates the caller's grid, so if the input must be preserved, use a visited bitmap instead and say so.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int NumIslands(char[][] grid)
    {
        if (grid == null || grid.Length == 0) return 0;

        int rows = grid.Length, cols = grid[0].Length, count = 0;

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++)
                if (grid[r][c] == '1')
                {
                    count++;
                    Sink(grid, r, c);     // remove the whole island from play
                }

        return count;
    }

    // Iterative BFS rather than recursive DFS: a 1000x1000 all-land grid would
    // recurse a million deep and overflow the stack.
    private static void Sink(char[][] grid, int startRow, int startCol)
    {
        int rows = grid.Length, cols = grid[0].Length;
        var queue = new Queue<(int r, int c)>();

        grid[startRow][startCol] = '0';
        queue.Enqueue((startRow, startCol));

        int[] dr = { -1, 1, 0, 0 };
        int[] dc = { 0, 0, -1, 1 };

        while (queue.Count > 0)
        {
            var (r, c) = queue.Dequeue();
            for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++)
            {
                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];
                if (nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols) continue;
                if (grid[nr][nc] != '1') continue;

                grid[nr][nc] = '0';       // mark on enqueue, not on dequeue
                queue.Enqueue((nr, nc));
            }
        }
    }

    private static char[][] Parse(params string[] rows)
    {
        var g = new char[rows.Length][];
        for (int i = 0; i < rows.Length; i++) g[i] = rows[i].ToCharArray();
        return g;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(NumIslands(Parse(
            "11110",
            "11010",
            "11000",
            "00000")));

        Console.WriteLine(NumIslands(Parse(
            "11000",
            "11000",
            "00100",
            "00011")));

        Console.WriteLine(NumIslands(Parse("000", "000")));
        Console.WriteLine(NumIslands(Parse("1")));
        Console.WriteLine(NumIslands(Parse("101", "010", "101")));   // diagonals do not connect
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
1
3
0
1
5
```

**Logic**

A deep copy of a graph needs a map from original node to its clone. Traverse from the start node; on reaching a node for the first time, create its clone and record it in the map *before* recursing into its neighbours. That ordering is what stops a cycle from recursing forever: when the traversal comes back around, the map already holds the clone and it is returned immediately.

O(V + E) time and space. The same technique clones any object graph with cycles, which is why serialisers keep an identity map.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Node
{
    public int Val;
    public IList<Node> Neighbors = new List<Node>();
    public Node(int val) { Val = val; }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static Node CloneGraph(Node start)
    {
        if (start == null) return null;
        return Clone(start, new Dictionary<Node, Node>());
    }

    private static Node Clone(Node node, Dictionary<Node, Node> map)
    {
        if (map.TryGetValue(node, out var existing)) return existing;

        // Register the clone BEFORE recursing, or a cycle loops forever.
        var copy = new Node(node.Val);
        map[node] = copy;

        foreach (var neighbour in node.Neighbors)
            copy.Neighbors.Add(Clone(neighbour, map));

        return copy;
    }

    private static string Describe(Node start)
    {
        if (start == null) return "(null)";
        var seen = new SortedDictionary<int, List<int>>();
        var stack = new Stack<Node>();
        stack.Push(start);

        while (stack.Count > 0)
        {
            var n = stack.Pop();
            if (seen.ContainsKey(n.Val)) continue;
            seen[n.Val] = n.Neighbors.Select(x => x.Val).OrderBy(x => x).ToList();
            foreach (var nb in n.Neighbors) stack.Push(nb);
        }

        return string.Join(" ", seen.Select(kv => $"{kv.Key}:[{string.Join(",", kv.Value)}]"));
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        // 1 - 2
        // |   |
        // 4 - 3
        var n1 = new Node(1); var n2 = new Node(2);
        var n3 = new Node(3); var n4 = new Node(4);
        n1.Neighbors = new List<Node> { n2, n4 };
        n2.Neighbors = new List<Node> { n1, n3 };
        n3.Neighbors = new List<Node> { n2, n4 };
        n4.Neighbors = new List<Node> { n1, n3 };

        var copy = CloneGraph(n1);
        Console.WriteLine(Describe(copy));

        // It must be a genuine copy, not the same objects.
        Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(copy, n1));
        Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(copy.Neighbors[0], n2));

        Console.WriteLine(Describe(CloneGraph(new Node(7))));   // no neighbours
        Console.WriteLine(Describe(CloneGraph(null)));

        var self = new Node(9);
        self.Neighbors.Add(self);                                // self-loop
        Console.WriteLine(Describe(CloneGraph(self)));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
1:[2,4] 2:[1,3] 3:[2,4] 4:[1,3]
False
False
7:[]
(null)
9:[9]
```

**Logic**

Courses with prerequisites form a directed graph, and the schedule is completable exactly when that graph has no cycle. Kahn's algorithm answers it by construction: count how many prerequisites each course has, start with those that have none, and repeatedly remove a course and decrement its dependents. If every course is eventually removed, there was no cycle; if some remain with a non-zero count, they form one.

O(V + E) time and space. The same run also yields a valid ordering, which is the sequel to this problem.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool CanFinish(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites)
    {
        var dependents = new List<int>[numCourses];
        var indegree = new int[numCourses];

        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) dependents[i] = new List<int>();

        // pair = [course, prerequisite]: an edge prerequisite -> course.
        foreach (var pair in prerequisites)
        {
            dependents[pair[1]].Add(pair[0]);
            indegree[pair[0]]++;
        }

        var ready = new Queue<int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++)
            if (indegree[i] == 0) ready.Enqueue(i);

        int taken = 0;
        while (ready.Count > 0)
        {
            int course = ready.Dequeue();
            taken++;

            foreach (int next in dependents[course])
                if (--indegree[next] == 0) ready.Enqueue(next);
        }

        // Anything left never reached indegree 0, so it sits on a cycle.
        return taken == numCourses;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(CanFinish(2, new[] { new[] { 1, 0 } }));
        Console.WriteLine(CanFinish(2, new[] { new[] { 1, 0 }, new[] { 0, 1 } }));   // cycle
        Console.WriteLine(CanFinish(3, Array.Empty<int[]>()));                        // no edges
        Console.WriteLine(CanFinish(4, new[] {
            new[] { 1, 0 }, new[] { 2, 1 }, new[] { 3, 2 } }));                       // a chain
        Console.WriteLine(CanFinish(3, new[] {
            new[] { 0, 1 }, new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 2, 0 } }));                       // 3-cycle
        Console.WriteLine(CanFinish(1, new[] { new[] { 0, 0 } }));                    // self-loop
    }
}
