5
3
3
0
1
```

**Logic**

For each item there are two choices: leave it, keeping the best value for this capacity without it, or take it, adding its value to the best value for the remaining capacity using earlier items only. The "earlier items only" part is what makes it 0/1 rather than unbounded.

The two-dimensional table is O(items × capacity). It collapses to one row if the capacity loop runs *backwards*, because that guarantees each item is considered at most once. Running it forwards is the unbounded knapsack, where items may repeat.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    // One row, capacity descending: each item used at most once.
    public static int Knapsack(int[] weights, int[] values, int capacity)
    {
        var best = new int[capacity + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i < weights.Length; i++)
            for (int c = capacity; c >= weights[i]; c--)   // BACKWARDS: 0/1
                best[c] = Math.Max(best[c], best[c - weights[i]] + values[i]);

        return best[capacity];
    }

    // Same code, capacity ascending: items may be reused any number of times.
    public static int UnboundedKnapsack(int[] weights, int[] values, int capacity)
    {
        var best = new int[capacity + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i < weights.Length; i++)
            for (int c = weights[i]; c <= capacity; c++)   // FORWARDS: unbounded
                best[c] = Math.Max(best[c], best[c - weights[i]] + values[i]);

        return best[capacity];
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        int[] w = { 1, 3, 4, 5 };
        int[] v = { 1, 4, 5, 7 };

        Console.WriteLine(Knapsack(w, v, 7));              // 3 and 4: weight 7, value 9
        Console.WriteLine(UnboundedKnapsack(w, v, 7));     // same answer on this input

        // Where the loop direction actually shows: capacity 6 over two items.
        int[] w2 = { 2, 3 };
        int[] v2 = { 3, 4 };
        Console.WriteLine(Knapsack(w2, v2, 6));            // 2+3, each once: 7
        Console.WriteLine(UnboundedKnapsack(w2, v2, 6));   // 2+2+2, reused: 9

        Console.WriteLine(Knapsack(new[] { 5 }, new[] { 10 }, 4));   // does not fit
        Console.WriteLine(Knapsack(new int[0], new int[0], 10));
        Console.WriteLine(Knapsack(new[] { 2, 2, 2 }, new[] { 3, 3, 3 }, 5));
        Console.WriteLine(UnboundedKnapsack(new[] { 2 }, new[] { 3 }, 7));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
9
9
7
9
0
0
6
9
```

Lines three and four are the point: identical items and capacity, 7 when each item may be used once and 9 when they may be reused. The only difference between the two methods is the direction of the capacity loop.

**Logic**

The array splits into two equal-sum halves exactly when some subset sums to half the total. An odd total is impossible immediately, which is a free O(n) rejection.

Otherwise it is a 0/1 knapsack over booleans: `reachable[t]` means some subset sums to t. For each number, iterate the target downwards so each number is used at most once, marking `reachable[t]` true when `reachable[t - num]` was.

O(n × sum/2) time, O(sum/2) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool CanPartition(int[] nums)
    {
        int total = 0;
        foreach (int n in nums) total += n;

        if (total % 2 != 0) return false;   // an odd total cannot split evenly

        int target = total / 2;
        var reachable = new bool[target + 1];
        reachable[0] = true;                // the empty subset sums to zero

        foreach (int n in nums)
        {
            // Downwards, so each number contributes to at most one subset.
            for (int t = target; t >= n; t--)
                if (reachable[t - n]) reachable[t] = true;

            if (reachable[target]) return true;   // early exit once it is achievable
        }

        return reachable[target];
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(CanPartition(new[] { 1, 5, 11, 5 }));      // 11 and 1+5+5
        Console.WriteLine(CanPartition(new[] { 1, 2, 3, 5 }));       // total 11, odd
        Console.WriteLine(CanPartition(new[] { 2, 2, 2, 2 }));
        Console.WriteLine(CanPartition(new[] { 1, 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(CanPartition(new[] { 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(CanPartition(new[] { 3, 3, 3, 4, 5 }));    // total 18, target 9
    }
}
```

