`ABCB` is the case that matters: the letters are all present, but reaching the final B would mean visiting the same cell twice.

**Logic**

Greedy, not DP. Sweep left to right keeping the furthest index reachable so far. At each position, if it lies beyond that reach, it is unreachable and so is everything after it, so return false. Otherwise extend the reach to include what this position can reach.

O(n) time, O(1) space. The DP version marking reachability per index is O(n²) and unnecessary, because the reach is monotonic: once you can get somewhere, you can get anywhere before it.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool CanJump(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length == 0) return false;

        int reach = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            if (i > reach) return false;                 // a gap nothing can cross

            reach = Math.Max(reach, i + nums[i]);

            if (reach >= nums.Length - 1) return true;    // the end is already covered
        }

        return true;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(CanJump(new[] { 2, 3, 1, 1, 4 }));
        Console.WriteLine(CanJump(new[] { 3, 2, 1, 0, 4 }));   // stuck at the 0
        Console.WriteLine(CanJump(new[] { 0 }));               // already at the end
        Console.WriteLine(CanJump(new[] { 0, 1 }));            // cannot leave index 0
        Console.WriteLine(CanJump(new[] { 2, 0, 0 }));         // jumps over the zeros
        Console.WriteLine(CanJump(new[] { 1, 0, 1, 0 }));
    }
