A single `[0]` is True because you start at the last index already, while `[0,1]` is False because you can never leave index 0.

**Logic**

A breadth-first search that needs no queue. Every index reachable in k jumps forms a contiguous range, so track the end of the current range and the furthest index reachable from anywhere within it. When the scan arrives at the end of the current range, one jump has been consumed and the range extends to the furthest seen.

O(n) time, O(1) space. The loop stops one short of the end, because arriving at the last index means the jump has already been counted.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int Jump(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length <= 1) return 0;

        int jumps = 0;
        int currentEnd = 0;    // last index reachable with the jumps taken so far
        int furthest = 0;      // furthest reachable from anywhere in the current range

        // Stop before the last index: reaching it means the jump is already counted.
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length - 1; i++)
        {
            furthest = Math.Max(furthest, i + nums[i]);

            if (i == currentEnd)       // the current range is exhausted
            {
                jumps++;
                currentEnd = furthest;

                if (currentEnd >= nums.Length - 1) break;
            }
        }

        return jumps;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Jump(new[] { 2, 3, 1, 1, 4 }));    // 2 -> 3 -> end
        Console.WriteLine(Jump(new[] { 2, 3, 0, 1, 4 }));
        Console.WriteLine(Jump(new[] { 0 }));                 // already there
        Console.WriteLine(Jump(new[] { 1, 2 }));
        Console.WriteLine(Jump(new[] { 1, 1, 1, 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(Jump(new[] { 5, 1, 1, 1, 1 }));     // one big jump
    }
}
```

